      <div>
        <p class="profile-detail-region">Battle · Ancient Near East</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Mycale</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">27 or 28 August 479 BCE, the afternoon of the same day as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Battle of Plataea</a> according to Paul A. Rahe; Charles Hignett proposes late August</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">The slopes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-mycale">Mount Mycale</a> on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a>, opposite the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>; the exact site is disputed</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">An alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-city-states">Greek city-states</a> including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and Corinth, under the Spartan king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leotychidas-ii">Leotychides</a>, against the Persian Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a>, whose land force was led by Tigranes and whose fleet was led by Artaÿntes; the strengths on both sides are disputed</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Persian troops were routed and their camp stormed, and the Persian ships were captured and burned. Tigranes and Mardontes were killed, while Artaÿntes and Ithamitres escaped.</span></div>
        </div>
        <p class="empire-meta">7 min read</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Persian fleet, probably fewer than a third of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>'s 300 ships after the Phoenician ships were dismissed, was beached below Mycale and fenced with a palisade, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> attacked it there by landing their marines.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionian Greek</a> contingents in the Persian army turned on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> during the fighting, though Joshua Nudell argues this betrayal is overstated and perhaps invented.</li><li>Together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a>, the battle ended the second Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-persian-invasion-of-greece">invasion of Greece</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> then went over to the offensive.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Richard Evans notes that Mycale was the first battle in recorded history fought on a beach, and he estimates that the strip of beach was only 20 to 30 metres long at low tide.</p></aside>
    </section>

