    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">480 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">A Persian army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> defeats the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a> and Artemisium, and the Greek navy wins at Salamis</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Spring 479 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Greek fleet of about 110 ships gathers at Aegina under the Spartan king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leotychidas-ii">Leotychides</a> and moves to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delos">Delos</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 479 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">A delegation from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> tells the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> that the Ionian cities are ready to revolt, and the fleet sails for Samos</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Summer 479 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> withdraw from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> and beach their fleet below Mycale behind a palisaded camp</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 479 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> land, defeat the Persian force and burn its ships</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Autumn 479 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Greek fleet sails to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> and finds the pontoon bridges destroyed, and the Athenians besiege Sestos</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Late 479 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Chios, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> join the Hellenic alliance</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-mycale" data-v="afff0bd753" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The Greeks land and form two wings</b> The Greeks landed out of sight of the Persian camp and formed two wings. The left stood from the shore to the foothills of the mountain, the right on the hills. The Persians had a palisaded camp with the fleet beached behind it.</li><li><b>The Greek left meets the Persians alone</b> The Persians came out of their camp and set up their shield wall. The Greek left began fighting while the right was still crossing the hills.</li><li><b>The right comes up and breaks the shield wall</b> The other Greek wing, under heavy arrow fire, chose to fight at close quarters and broke the wall by shoving. Tigranes and Mardontes died in the fighting.</li><li><b>The Persians flee to their camp</b> The Persians broke their lines and escaped to their camp. The Greek soldiers followed them in, and most of the Persian troops fled from the camp. The ethnic Persians grouped together and fought those who entered.</li><li><b>The left outflanks the camp</b> The Greek left arrived, outflanked the camp and fell on the rear of the Persians who remained, which completed the rout.</li><li><b>The Milesians turn on the Persians</b> The Milesians guarding the passes first misdirected the fleeing Persians, who ended up back among the Greek troops, and then began killing them.</li><li><b>The fleet and fortifications are burned</b> The Greeks burned the Persian fleet after a heavily contested fight with the Persian marines, once the loot had been taken from the ships and placed on the beach. The fortifications were burnt too.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xerxes-i.jpg" alt="Xerxes I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:National_Museum_of_Iran_Darafsh_(785).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Darafsh, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a> had invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in 480 BCE. His army defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a>, and his fleet forced the Greek fleet to withdraw at Artemisium, after which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> burned Boeotian cities and took the evacuated city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. At Salamis the Greek navy won a victory that prevented the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, and Xerxes retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> with a minor portion of his army, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardonius">Mardonius</a> with most of it to finish the war the next year.</p><p>The Persian fleet was stationed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> to defend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> and avert an Ionian revolt. Its morale was breaking, and its crews were waiting anxiously for news of Mardonius. Charles Hignett argues that only a Persian victory on land in Greece could sustain Persian rule in Ionia. In the spring of 479 BCE the Greek fleet of about 110 ships lay at Aegina under the Spartan king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leotychidas-ii">Leotychides</a>. Six people from Chios who had failed to overthrow their tyrant Strattis reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> and persuaded the fleet to move to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delos">Delos</a>, but the Greeks hesitated to go further.</p><p>In July a delegation from Samos under Hegesistratos told them that the Ionian cities were eager to revolt and that the Persian fleet was in poor repair. Leotychides and the council of war decided to take the chance and sailed for Samos. Historians differ on how risky this was: Hignett says Leotychides was gambling on a battle at a place the Persians would choose, while Jack Balcer stresses that the fleet's original task had only been to keep the Persian fleet from reinforcing Mardonius.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Mycale&#8221; cites where it covers Background, Prelude:</p><ul><li>Charles Hignett, Xerxes' Invasion of Greece (Clarendon Press, 1963)</li><li>Andrew Robert Burn, Persia and the Greeks. The Defence of the West, c. 546-478 B. C. (Gerald Duckworth &amp; Co., 1984)</li><li>George Cawkwell, The Greek Wars: The Failure of Persia (Oxford University Press, 2005)</li><li>Michael A. Flower, Herodotus. Histories Book IX (Cambridge University Press, 2002)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herodotus.jpg" alt="Herodotus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marble_bust_of_Herodotos_MET_DT11742_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> gives the Persian fleet that wintered at Cyme as 300 ships, but the Phoenician ships were dismissed before the battle. Hignett finds 300 too many even with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a>, and Peter Green and John Barron both estimate about 100 ships after their departure. Richard Evans, by contrast, estimates 200. Tigranes commanded the land force, and Artaÿntes was joint commander of the fleet and appointed his nephew Ithamitres as the third commander. Herodotus puts the Persian army at 60,000, which Hignett says his own narrative contradicts. Hignett and William Shepherd think Tigranes had about 10,000 men, Green 6,000 plus 4,000 marines from the fleet, and Evans about 4,000 Persian combatants in all. Shepherd notes that he had no cavalry.</p><p>Andrew Robert Burn estimates 110 Greek ships and notes that the figure of 250 is given only by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephorus">Ephorus</a>. Herodotus gives no order of battle. Shepherd thinks <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> supplied a third of the ships, and Evans reckons 70 Athenian, 8 Spartan, 20 Corinthian, 3 Troezenian and 8 Sicyonian. Estimates of the marines run from 2,000 to 3,000 (George Cawkwell) to 3,300 (Michael Flower and John Marincola, and Hignett). Shepherd allows at most 25,000 Greek soldiers, most of them oarsmen serving as light infantry, while Evans counts 1,100 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplites</a>. Flower and Marincola think <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leotychidas-ii">Leotychides</a> and Xanthippus, the Athenian commander, probably had equal powers and decided by majority vote.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> took the arms of the Samians in their army, whom they found unreliable, and sent the Milesians to guard the mountain passes, suspecting they wished to defect.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Mycale&#8221; cites where it covers Persians, Greeks:</p><ul><li>Andrew Robert Burn, Persia and the Greeks. The Defence of the West, c. 546-478 B. C. (Gerald Duckworth &amp; Co., 1984)</li><li>George Cawkwell, The Greek Wars: The Failure of Persia (Oxford University Press, 2005)</li><li>Charles Hignett, Xerxes' Invasion of Greece (Clarendon Press, 1963)</li><li>Peter Green, The Greco-Persian Wars (University of California Press, 1996)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea.jpg" alt="Battle of Plataea" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Battle of Plataea</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scene_of_the_Battle_of_Plataea.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Steeple Davis, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> heard that the Greek fleet was approaching, they sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> to Mycale, possibly because they had decided they could not fight at sea, and in the chaos the Samians freed 500 Athenian captives. The Persians beached their ships behind rocks, built abatis with recently cut wood, set stakes on the ramparts and expected a siege. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> camped at Kalamoi, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraion-of-samos">Heraion of Samos</a>, then moved on Mycale, calling on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionians">Ionians</a> to revolt, and landed out of sight of the enemy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> reports a rumour that spread among them of a Greek victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> earlier that day.</p><p>The Greeks probably formed two wings. On the left stood the Athenians, Corinthians, Sicyonians and Troezenians, about half the army, between the shore and the foothills. On the right were the Spartans and the other contingents on rough ground in the hills. The left began fighting while the right was still crossing the hills. The Persians came out and set up a shield wall. Green argues that sending the Athenian flank forward alone was a ruse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leotychidas-ii">Leotychides</a>.</p><p>The Greek right, under heavy arrow fire, closed in, and its contingents broke the wall by shoving. Tigranes and Mardontes died, and the Persians fled to their camp. Most of the fugitives ran on, but the ethnic Persians stood and fought until the Greek left outflanked the camp and fell on their rear. Herodotus says the disarmed Samians then joined the Greeks, the other Ionian contingents followed, and the Milesians at the passes first misdirected the fleeing Persians and then killed them. The Greeks burned the Persian ships after a hard fight with the Persian marines, and the camp.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Mycale&#8221; cites where it covers The battle:</p><ul><li>Andrew Robert Burn, Persia and the Greeks. The Defence of the West, c. 546-478 B. C. (Gerald Duckworth &amp; Co., 1984)</li><li>Charles Hignett, Xerxes' Invasion of Greece (Clarendon Press, 1963)</li><li>William Shepherd, Plataea 479 BC: The Most Glorious Victory Ever Seen (Osprey Publishing, 2012)</li><li>Peter Green, The Greco-Persian Wars (University of California Press, 1996)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> gives no figures for the dead, saying only that losses were heavy on both sides. The Sicyonians suffered especially and lost their general Perilaus. Green estimates the Persian dead at 4,000. Mardontes and Tigranes were killed, but Artaÿntes and Ithamitres escaped. Hermolycus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> was thought the most gallant Greek, and the Athenians were thought the most courageous contingent, followed by Corinth, Troezen and Sicyon. Burn and Muhammad Dandamayev hold that the destruction of the Persian fleet gave the Greek fleets complete freedom of movement, and Dandamayev says the Persian and Median marines and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> of Tigranes' army were almost completely massacred.</p><p>With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> and Mycale the second Persian invasion was over. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> and then sailed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> to break down the pontoon bridges, but found them destroyed. The Peloponnesians sailed home. The Athenians stayed to attack the Chersonesos and, after a long siege, took Sestos, the strongest town of the region. Chios, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> and Samos joined the Hellenic alliance and their Persian garrisons were eliminated. The Greeks could not subdue <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> entirely and freed only the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> islands, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and Macedonia broke with the Persians, as did the coastal areas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> and the Greek communities from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troad">Troad</a> to Caria.</p><p>The Peloponnesians proposed moving the Ionian Greeks to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and giving them the lands of the medizers, but the plan was dropped, and Xanthippus said Athens would not let its settlements be abandoned.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Mycale&#8221; cites where it covers Aftermath, Spoils:</p><ul><li>Andrew Robert Burn, Persia and the Greeks. The Defence of the West, c. 546-478 B. C. (Gerald Duckworth &amp; Co., 1984)</li><li>Jack Martin Balcer, The Persian Conquest of the Greeks 545 - 450 BC (Universitatsverlag Konstanz, 1995)</li><li>M. A. Dandamaev, A Political History of the Achaemenid Empire (E. J. Brill, 1989)</li><li>Joshua P. Nudell, Accustomed to Obedience? Classical Ionia and the Aegean World, 480–294 BCE (University of Michigan Press, 2023)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus.jpg" alt="Diodorus Siculus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diodoro_siculo_-_storico_di_Agira.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The main source is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, who tells the battle in Book Nine of his Histories. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> also gives an account, which Flower and Marincola find unreliable and mixed with the claims of his main source, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephorus">Ephorus of Cyme</a>. Most ancient writers gave the battle little thought. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> is the only one to describe the Athenians as proud of it, and he notes Corinthian gallantry. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> did not rate it as highly as Salamis or Artemisium.</p><p>Modern historians have found Herodotus's account shaped by its design. Detlev Fehling and Shepherd note its likeness to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a>, and others compare it with Artemisium, Lade and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a>. Hignett thinks the similarities natural. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Asheri holds that Herodotus told the battle from a religious rather than a military viewpoint, and Henry Immerwahr that its Athenian bias comes from a pro-Athenian source, probably Hellanicus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a>. Evans suggests <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> as a model. Nudell thinks the Ionian betrayal of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> may have been invented to save the reputation of Greek states that had fought for them.</p><p>The site is disputed, and proposals include Domatia (Theodor Wiegand), near Ak Bogaz (Johannes Kromayer), Atburgazı (Shepherd), Güllübahçe (Jan Zacharias van Rookhuijzen) and a place between Atburgazı and Yuvaca (Dietram Müller). Iain McDougall doubts that the Persian ships were burned, since ships were valuable loot, while Asheri says the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> lacked crews to man them.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Mycale&#8221; cites where it covers Analysis, Historiography, Ships:</p><ul><li>Michael A. Flower, Herodotus. Histories Book IX (Cambridge University Press, 2002)</li><li>Andrew Robert Burn, Persia and the Greeks. The Defence of the West, c. 546-478 B. C. (Gerald Duckworth &amp; Co., 1984)</li><li>George Cawkwell, The Greek Wars: The Failure of Persia (Oxford University Press, 2005)</li><li>H. T. Wallinga, Xerxes' Greek Adventure: The Naval Perspective (Brill, 2005)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/demosthenes.jpg" alt="Demosthenes" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes">Demosthenes</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Demosthenes_Polyeuctos_Louvre_Ma237_n01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Polyeuctus, CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Cawkwell calls the battle a very minor affair, but says its results were large because it led to an Ionian revolt, and Burn calls it relatively small. Iain Spence argues that it confirmed Greek command of the sea and that the defection of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> states helped the Greek naval campaigns, leading to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delian-league">Delian League</a> and the naval empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. Persian borders were then fixed, and later Persian invasions became rare. Bury argues that Spartan indecision left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> to the Delian League under Athens, and that the Spartans' hesitation and Athenian resolve fed the rivalry of the fifth century BCE. Nudell sees in the debate over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionians">Ionians</a> a foreshadowing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>.</p><p>Spoils were displayed at Delphi, where the Athenians took credit in an inscription without naming their allies. Barron suggests that cables from the Persian bridges adorned the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> Nike, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes">Demosthenes</a> thought the statue of Athena Promachos was paid for by the loot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> and Mycale. Vitruvius says masts and spars of the Persian ships went into the roof of the Odeon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a>.</p><p>Memory of the battle is uneven. Flower and Marincola note that no Athenian orator celebrated it, perhaps because a Spartan led it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> C. Yates notes that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> and Chios, with strong claims, are missing from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serpent-column">Serpent Column</a>. An epigram from Megara tells of Megarians who fought and suffered losses, though no account of the battle names them.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Mycale in August 479 BCE a Greek force landed from its fleet under the Spartan king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leotychidas-ii">Leotychides</a> and routed the Persian troops beached below the mountain, killing their commanders Tigranes and Mardontes and burning their ships. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> it ended the second Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-persian-invasion-of-greece">invasion of Greece</a>.</em></p>

